Make the speaker amplifier follow output selection, not stream lifecycle,
add 3.5 mm jack detection, and drive the DAC mux on route change.

Measured on Device #1 on 2026-08-11:

  - The two pops reproduce on pure digital silence, so they are made by the
    enable/disable sequence and not by audio content.
  - They appear as an identical doublet at BOTH amplifier enable and disable.
    The AW8736 "Mode 3" pulse train runs on only one of those two transitions,
    so it cannot be what makes them sound alike. The pulse train is removed
    here for fidelity with the vendor, not as the fix.
  - Toggling the codec's MFP2 mute mid-stream is completely silent, so neither
    half of the doublet is the codec mute.

What is left is the amplifier transition itself. The vendor never power-cycles
the amplifier per stream: mt_soc_codec_63xx.c guards the enable behind the
codec device power state, so a second stream start touches nothing. This driver
enabled it in .startup and disabled it in .shutdown, so every play/stop cycle
produced a doublet at each end.

Amplifier state becomes (output == Speaker) AND (a stream is running, or one
stopped less than MT8163_BISCUIT_AMP_IDLE_MS ago). The idle grace period is
what removes the pops between tracks while still letting the amplifier power
down when nothing is playing, so the device does not sit amplifying its own
noise floor. An amp_on flag makes the transition idempotent, mirroring the
vendor's device-power guard.

Transitions use a single clean level change with the vendor's timings:
msleep(25) warm-up after enable (stock SPK_WARM_UP_TIME) and udelay(500) after
disable. Biscuit takes its amplifier gain from hardware, not from a pulse
train - the four aud_pins_extamp_gain0..3 states exist in the vendor DTS but
are bound by nothing, in the source drop and in all three shipped DTBs alike,
so AudDrv_GPIO_EXTAMP_Gain_Set() fails on every call.

The DAC mux (GPIO124) is now driven on route change. It was acquired at probe
and never referenced again, so it stayed latched to the speaker path and the
amplifier kept being fed whichever output the UCM selected - the reason muting
MFP2 never cured the whine on the jack.

Jack detection matches the vendor: GPIO26, high means inserted, which
accdet_amzn.c reads as a plain GPIO level rather than through the MT6323
accessory comparator. Insertion switches the output automatically. The
userspace half of the archived port (99-biscuit-hp-switch.rules) cannot work
on mainline, because it keys on the Android switch class, which was never
upstreamed.

